Range main...HEAD at 5a467d8..8ceb3eb, main at 5a467d8. TWO dedicated read-only
reviewers, both with the pristine build and objdump, on complementary briefs:
one on the surviving code's logic, the documentation's claims and the
measurement's reasoning; one purely on the deletion sweep -- what was taken away
and what still expects it. Two rounds of fixes (66dd160, 8ceb3eb). Every finding
verified against the code before acting; two were NOT upheld and are recorded
with the citation that refutes them.

The note is on the tip and not on 4479ed1, deliberately: reviewer 1 pointed out
mid-review that the worktree was dirty with round-1's fix, so a note on the
commit it read would attest a gate met on content that had since changed. The
range above is what the two of them read plus the two fix commits.

BOTH REVIEWERS INDEPENDENTLY RETURNED THE SAME HIGH, which is how the last two
landings' worst findings arrived too.

HIGH -- NO POSED UNIT CASTS INTO THE VULKAN CAST-SHADOW MAP, and this landing
documented the opposite. `tagpu_posedraw_depth_begin` held the only
`s_depthOn = 1` in the tree; with it deleted the chain is unbroken -- casts 0,
depthOn 0, s_ncast never increments, `tagpu_vk_unit_cast` returns at `!s_ncast`
(:2579), and `build_cast_pipeline` (only caller :2587, inside that function) is
never reached. The `pose_depth` pipeline the header called PORTED is never
built. The census that would catch it cannot: `otherCasters`' unit-side
incrementers have no callers, so on an ordinary frame it is 0 - 0 and the map is
published as complete. NOT a regression from this landing -- it dates to 11-3 --
but this landing deleted the last possible setter and then called it ported,
which is the sentence most likely to stop the next session looking. Corrected in
five places.

MEDIUM, AND THE ONE THAT CHANGED THE LANDING -- the measurement localised the
loss to our own pipeline and attributed it to the engine, and the control that
separates the two had not been run. It is one relaunch: the same fixture on
renderer=gdi, where the engine detours install identically and `live()` is 0,
but none of the Vulkan composite exists. RUN: the commander is PRESENT on gdi,
window capture and `tacli shot` the same picture. So round 1's conclusion --
"the engine's per-unit rasterise is invisible work" -- is FALSE. The loss is
OURS, in the Vulkan composite path, and fixable. The 4b-2 comfort is true on gdi
and false on Vulkan, and `tagpu_posedraw_live()` returning 0 is load-bearing
rather than merely safe: a 1 would take the engine's copy away too.

MEDIUM -- my round-1 fix itself overclaimed: `otherCasters` has THREE writers,
the third (`tagpu_shadow.c:473`, the heightfield path) live and reachable, so
"0 - 0, the guard reports success" is false in general. Narrowed to what holds.

MEDIUM -- "there is no `tacli disarm`" was wrong and taught a hand-rolled `rm`
over `tacli arm <i> <lever>=off`, which unlinks the file. MEDIUM -- the capture
recipe matched window titles by substring, so `tacli:play1` matches
`tacli:play10`. Both corrected in ta-drive.

LOW x7 -- stale citations this landing created or inherited, a log string still
promising 8bpp units, a source-vs-object uniform count (44 vs 25, both now
labelled), two wrong line numbers in my own round-1 chain (the reviewers
disagreed; the source settled it), and dead state left behind: the slant and
wire counters with their two unreachable branches, the PD_R_* macros, and
`unit_ok`'s `!tagpu_vk_owns_present() && !m->vao` -- a lane test in the file
whose own rule this landing is named for.

NOT UPHELD (1) -- that dropping the `tagpu_vk_owns_present()` wrapper from
`tagpu_posedraw_ready()` changes behaviour where the lane is ST_READY without
owning present. Not reachable: `tagpu_vk.c:509-512` says nothing but
`render_vk.c` drives this lane, `tagpu_vk_own_present()` has one caller before
the frame loop, `s_ownWin` is never cleared, and `tagpu_vk_max_uniform_range()`
returns 0 unless the lane is ST_READY. The second reviewer reached the same
conclusion independently and added the gdi case.

NOT UPHELD (2) -- the stale citation in `tools/spirv-gen.py`. Fixing it FAILS
THE BUILD: `tool_hash()` is the SHA-256 of the whole file and is one of three
hashes in every generated header, so a comment edit makes `spirv-check` reject
all thirteen and demand a glslang regeneration. Measured by doing it and
reverting; recorded in the gaps instead.

VERIFIED BY RUNNING IT, on the final binary, md5-matched to the build:
`posedraw: armed - no rasteriser of its own, pose block 14336 bytes`,
`units=7 pieces=30` in the hand-over (so `unit_ok` still passes records after
its dead line went), `vk: census ... unit=1`, `OWND target=all skipped=0
passed=64246`, and the presented frame pixel-identical to the pre-fix build.

